<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Social War (91–87 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">91–<a href="/years/87-bce">87 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Marsic War, Italian War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Social War was the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Italian allies, led by the Marsi and Samnites, who demanded Roman citizenship. It began with the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> in 91 BCE. Rome won on the battlefield but granted citizenship to loyal and then to all Italians south of the Po, turning the allied peoples and towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into Roman municipia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce.jpg" alt="Social War (91–87 BCE)" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Social_war_(Roman_and_insurgent_territory).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Social War</b> of 91–88 BCE forced Rome to grant citizenship to the Italian allies, and in 88 BCE Lucius Cornelius Sulla marched his army on Rome, beginning civil wars that ended with his dictatorship and the proscriptions of 82–81 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 90 BCE, after the outbreak of the <b>Social War</b>, Rome granted citizenship to the allies who had not rebelled, among them the Etruscans, and their cities became Roman municipalities.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First <b>Italian War</b>, a French force of gendarmes and Swiss pikemen routed a larger Spanish and Neapolitan army at Seminara in Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The towns became Roman municipia after the <b>Social War</b>, and Daunian identity faded into Roman Apulia.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li><li><blockquote>They joined the Italian rebels in the <b>Social War</b> of 91–88 BCE, then received citizenship and were absorbed into Roman Italy.</blockquote><a href="/state/frentani">Frentani</a></li><li><blockquote>It became a Roman municipium after the <b>Social War</b> and declined in late antiquity.</blockquote><a href="/state/heraclea-lucania">Heraclea Lucania</a></li></ul></section>
